  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nA weak split flow pattern begins to set up today with low \namplitude troughing across the Arctic Circle while broad ridging\ncontinues to occupy the southern U.S. Pleasant summertime \nconditions will persist today as surface high pressure over the \nNortheast supports sunny skies and precipitation free conditions.\n\n\nConditions turn more active this weekend for the Great Lakes region \nas the aforementioned 500mb trough begins to deepen, developing \nclosed low pressure over Hudson Bay and extending the trough axis \ninto the Upper Midwest. A surface clipper progresses along the 60th \nparallel and drags a frontal boundary across Canada's Southern \nProvinces this Saturday while weakening. A few showers will likely \nbe observed mainly across Michigan\u2019s Upper Peninsula, but \nlittle QPF is expected. The best chance of widespread \nprecipitation remains on Monday as another surface low digs \nacross the Great Lakes Region. Latest guidance continues to \nincrease probabilities of QPF, along with the potential for \nsome stronger embedded storms developing due to increasing \nafternoon instability and favorable lapse rates building across \nthe region.\n\nForecast Details: \n\nToday through Sunday: Conditions remain pretty benign today as \nhigh pressure is positioned directly overhead. Subsidence aloft \nwill continue sunny skies, calm winds, and highs in the upper \n70s/low 80s for most locations. Only chance of rain this weekend\nremains on Saturday as a surface low develops over Northern \nAlberta while an attached front drags across the southern \nCanadian provinces. This frontal passage will weaken with time, \ndelivering light amounts of moisture to the U.S./Canadian \nBorder. Only Eastern Upper and the Tip of the Mitt is expected \nto observe measurable rainfall Saturday afternoon and evening. \nChippewa and Mackinac counties can expect a tenth of an inch or \nless while localized areas south of the Bridge will only observe\na trace. Sunday will likely remain dry, but with an approaching\nshortwave combined with some lingering low level moisture, one \nor two showers remain possible.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Shortwave troughing currently over the Canadian \nWest Coast progresses eastward this weekend. Surface low pressure \ndevelops Lee of the Rockies and digs across Lake Michigan this \nMonday. Latest guidance depicts moderate instability building in the \nafternoon, PWATs are climatologically high, and CAPE profiles \nsupport a round of showers with embedded convection. Best location \nfor active weather remains southwest of the CWA as SPC places the \nsouthern Michigan/Chicago Region in a day 4 outlook, but some \nstronger storms could still produce localized heavy rainfall and \npotential severe weather. The highest probabilities of QPF remain \nbetween a quarter to half an inch.  \n\nWednesday and Thursday Outlook: Surface high pressure appears to \nbuild on the backside of this system as it heads downstream, \nreturning sunny skies and dry weather through the end of the \nforecast period. Subsequent cold air advection will drop dew points \nand temperatures by Wednesday, resulting in highs in the mid 70s to \nnear 80s Wednesday through Friday.",
